WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.461
Two paper money streams enter transparent
vessels.

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:09.083
One begins with a strong steady flow,
while the other starts more slowly and

00:00:09.083 --> 00:00:10.689
gathers speed over time.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:20.784
The steady stream builds an early lead.
Its vessel fills faster at first,

00:00:20.784 --> 00:00:25.522
showing why a strong beginning can matter
even when another flow grows.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.545
The growing stream reaches the same
current rate, then becomes faster.

00:00:34.545 --> 00:00:38.439
Yet its vessel remains behind, because its
earlier contributions were smaller

00:00:38.439 --> 00:00:39.856
than the steady stream.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.979
Later, the accumulated amounts finally
meet.

00:00:47.979 --> 00:00:53.026
The past shortfall has been recovered, and
the growing stream can now move

00:00:53.026 --> 00:00:55.356
ahead over the remaining comparison
period.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.510
A movable timeline selects the comparison
date.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.004
Earlier and later horizons reveal
different leaders, so the answer must

00:01:08.004 --> 00:01:11.272
include the period over which
contributions are measured.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.191
The two vessels pause at the end of the
year.

00:01:19.191 --> 00:01:23.085
Which stream has contributed more, and
what calculation compares the complete

00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:24.856
paths rather than one instant?
